    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-51269",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula",
      "problem": "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,2].$",
      "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula:\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[2\\sqrt{x}]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1+4x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27-1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
