    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122365",
      "knowledge_point_id": "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral",
      "problem": "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?",
      "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=36{e}^{6x}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 6{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 6\\int {e}^{3x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm 2{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=2{e}^{3x}+1.$",
